  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 1218 AM MDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nAn impressively deep upper low/trough is forecast to move inland\nThu/Fri over the Pacific NW and pull the monsoon moisture plume \nback north over central and western NM, bringing well above \naverage PWATs and an uptick in convective coverage. Locally heavy\nrainfall and localized flash flooding will continue to be the \nprimary threat, especially across western NM. A moderate heat risk\nwill persist through the end of the week across the eastern\nplains, where pressure heights will trend slowly upward as the\nupper high begins to build back west toward NM. The upper high is\nforecast to center over far eastern NM and the OK/TX Panhandles\nover the weekend, focusing daily rounds of storms across the\nwestern and northern mountains. The upper high will be slow to\nback out of the region toward the east next week, but may allow\nfor some tropical moisture to get picked up by a west coast trough\nforecast to shift into the Intermountain West. This development \nwould favor western NM for daily rounds of storms initially, with \nthe moisture plume shifting into central NM by mid week.",
